item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q021_nad_binding_convergent_evolution","answer":"In the current SCOPe 2.08 base release (latest reported periodic update: 2.08-2026-08-16), NAD/NADP binding occurs in structurally and evolutionarily distinct groups—for example, the NAD(P)-binding Rossmann fold c.2 (SUNID 51734), the FAD/NAD(P)-binding fold c.3 (SUNID 51904), the ferredoxin-reductase-like C-terminal NADP-linked fold c.25 (SUNID 52342), and the NAD-binding domain of HMG-CoA reductase superfamily d.58.20 (SUNID 55035). Repeated recruitment of NAD or NADP across different folds—and even different structural classes—therefore supports convergent evolution driven by the same cofactor chemistry, not descent from one NAD-binding ancestor. Conversely, proteins within one SCOPe superfamily are inferred to have probable common ancestry and represent divergence, although their substrates and reactions may have changed.

The hierarchy sets the evidential strength: a family normally indicates a relatively close evolutionary relationship; a superfamily indicates probable, potentially remote homology; a fold records shared architecture/topology but does not by itself establish common ancestry; and a class is still broader and carries essentially no homology claim. Thus neither a common fold alone nor a shared NAD(P)-binding function licenses homology.

Cofactor recognition is especially prone to convergence because NAD and NADP present the same limited adenine, ribose, pyrophosphate, and nicotinamide features to every protein. Short glycine-rich loops, phosphate-binding patterns, catalytic residues, or geometrically similar active sites can consequently arise independently under chemical and steric constraints. Such local similarities are too short and functionally selected to outweigh incompatible global topology or an absence of statistically significant sequence/structure correspondence. Homology should instead be supported by coherent whole-domain evidence: significant sequence-profile or structural alignment, matching topology and domain boundaries, conserved residue networks beyond the ligand-contacting site, and, where appropriate, phylogenetic or genomic-context evidence. Biochemical evidence is separately needed to transfer a precise reaction or substrate specificity, because even genuine superfamily homologs can have different functions. Likewise, a template from another NAD(P)-binding fold is only an analogy and should not be used as a homologous template or as a basis for function transfer; even a same-superfamily template requires checks of alignment, coverage, catalytic residues, insertions, and oligomeric context.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge","/api/v1/","/api/v1/openapi.yaml","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD&mode=prefix&limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=Rossmann&mode=prefix&limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD&mode=prefix&limit=500","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NADP&mode=token&limit=500","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD%28P%29-binding&mode=auto&limit=100"]}]}
